    {
      "id": "exhaustive-explorer",
      "name": "Exhaustive explorer",
      "parent": "semantic-core",
      "category": "logical",
      "runsIn": "both",
      "purpose": "Explores every configuration of a scenario reachable within a bound, over every order of delivery of simultaneous events and every subset of the scenario's faults, with state deduplication. It reports that a state invariant holds in all N of them or returns a counterexample run, and it checks the temporal claims never, precedes and eventually on every complete run (a run ends at quiescence, at the step limit or in a failed step) by searching the product of the explorer's state with a small monitor. The explorer is defined in the Lean semantics and proved sound there; a faster explorer in the Rust engine (WG-4650) is to be checked differentially against it.",
      "responsibilities": [
        "Define the nondeterminism a scenario admits as the successor function of the behaviour calculus (Exec.successors): which ready entry is delivered next, which draw of each sampled delay is taken, and which subset of the scenario's faults acts in the run; it is sound and complete for the calculus's step relation (successors_sound, successors_complete)",
        "Explore breadth-first with a visited set under a bound on steps and a budget of states (exploreB over Exec.gen), and return holds with the number N of distinct calculus states visited, a counterexample (picks into the successor lists), or incomplete when the budget runs out",
        "Prove that holds, returned only on an empty frontier, means the invariant holds in every reachable state of the calculus with no bound (explore_holds_invariant), and that a counterexample replays through the successor function to a state where the property fails (explore_cex_real), with only Lean's standard axioms",
        "Report \"verified by Lean for all N configurations\" distinctly from trace-level checks",
        "Define the temporal forms never, precedes, eventually and response within a time bound over finite runs of the calculus (BehavCheck.lean: FinSat, monitor), and check them with the generic breadth-first search over the product of the calculus with a monitor (ProveB.lean: tsysB, tcheckB)",
        "Prove the monitor agrees with the definition of each form on every finite run (monitor_sound), that a holds of the temporal check covers every reachable product node (tcheckB_holds) and that a counterexample is a real path of the product to a violating node (tcheckB_cex); paths cut off by the bound or the state budget are reported incomplete, never holds"
      ],
      "unknowns": [
        "The engine input (format version 1) carries no bound of a scenario's fault model, so every subset of the scenario's faults is explored; the concurrency bounds of fault models are not applied yet.",
        "A fault acts for the whole run or not at all: a fault without an occurrence cannot act on some deliveries of its event and not on others.",
        "Breadth-first order makes a counterexample one of the fewest steps, but that is not proved.",
        "The Rust explorer for large state spaces is not built yet (planned: WG-4650); it will be checked differentially against this one.",
        "Evaluation errors and failed steps end a path without being reported as such: a configuration whose invariant cannot be evaluated counts as a violation, and a failed step has no successor.",
        "Format 2 inputs are not explored yet (planned: WG-4664, WG-4715). The specified relation adds internal entries to the simultaneous-order choice, the new fault kinds to the mask, and one successor per choice value and per instance of an any route; sampled draws are fixed by a seed named first in the result, and a result is shown as verified only when the scenario has no sampled site. Scenarios with workloads need a short horizon and maxArrivals, and keyed populations of thousands of clients are beyond reach. When a timed fault acts is not explored (WG-4704).",
        "Temporal claims are checked without the claim's negatives and without the trace check's vacuity rules (a never whose matcher cannot occur in the scenario holds here without a note).",
        "A violating prefix of a never or precedes claim is reported as the counterexample, not extended to a complete run; any extension violates it too, but that is not a theorem."
      ],
      "implementation": {
        "status": "manual",
        "modules": [
          "formal/Exegra/Core/BehavCheck.lean",
          "formal/Exegra/Search.lean"
        ],
        "notes": "formal/Exegra/Core/BehavCheck.lean: exploreB over the calculus scheduler's successor function, explore_holds_invariant and explore_cex_real, runs (IsRun), the temporal forms (TForm, FinSat), the monitor and monitor_sound. formal/Exegra/Search.lean: the generic breadth-first search over a node type with search_sound and search_counterexample_real. formal/Exegra/ProveB.lean: the claim invariants of a format-2 input over the calculus state (claimInvB), the product with a monitor (tsysB), tcheckB, tcheckB_holds, tcheckB_cex, the verdicts a generated proof file states (verdictOf, verdictOf_holds, tverdictOf) and the exegra-lean explore and temporal protocols. Axioms pinned with #guard_msgs. Format 1, and with it Explore.lean and Temporal.lean, was removed (WG-5681).",
        "language": "lean"
      },
      "tags": [
        "verdict-kernel"
      ]
    },
