    {
      "id": "formal-semantics",
      "name": "Formal semantics (Lean 4)",
      "parent": "semantic-core",
      "category": "logical",
      "runsIn": "both",
      "purpose": "The definition of the behavior language: the engine input, configurations, the expression language and the step relation, written in Lean 4 as an executable reference that the Rust engine is checked against, with its basic properties proved.",
      "responsibilities": [
        "Evaluate a run to a trace in the documented format",
        "Prove determinism of the step function, at most one fired transition per behavior per step, and termination under the step limit",
        "Serve as the reference for differential checking of the Rust engine on goldens and generated inputs",
        "Run, as the engine of the application (engine.run and exegra-lean), the implementation Exegra.Impl (formal/Exegra/Impl/): expressions parsed at most once per run, arithmetic evaluated on unboxed floats, a step counter instead of measuring the list of steps, and an in-place JSON writer; Exegra.Impl.runAnyValue_eq proves it equal to the definition Exegra.runAnyValue on every input, so the theorems about the definition hold of what runs"
      ],
      "implementation": {
        "status": "manual",
        "modules": [
          "formal/lakefile.toml",
          "formal/lake-manifest.json",
          "formal/lean-toolchain",
          "formal/Exegra/Expr.lean",
          "formal/Exegra/Impl/Json.lean",
          "formal/Exegra/Impl/Refinement.lean",
          "formal/Exegra/Impl/Semantics.lean",
          "formal/Exegra/Input.lean",
          "formal/Exegra/Json.lean",
          "formal/Exegra/Number.lean",
          "formal/Exegra/Trace.lean",
          "formal/Exegra/Value.lean",
          "formal/Exegra/Format2B.lean",
          "tools/gen-engine-inputs.js"
        ],
        "notes": "A Lake project (Lean 4, toolchain pinned in formal/lean-toolchain, standard library only). Exegra/Core/ is the behaviour calculus and its scheduler (Behav, BehavExec, BehavRename, BehavCheck); Exegra/Elab.lean elaborates a format-2 input into it; Exegra/Format2.lean is the legacy format-2 interpreter for inputs outside the fragment; Exegra/Format2B.lean dispatches (V2.runInput: traceOfB for elaborated inputs, the legacy traceOf otherwise, marked \"semantics\": \"legacy\") and is the entry point Exegra.runAnyValue, which refuses version 1; Value, Json, Number, Expr, Input and Trace are the shared parts. Main is the exegra-lean executable (engine input JSON on stdin, trace JSON on stdout; --legacy runs the legacy interpreter, for the differential check). npm run build:formal runs lake build in formal/; formal/.lake/ is build output, ignored by git and outside the traceability surface. tools/differential.js compares the native Rust engine with exegra-lean on the goldens and on seeded inputs from tools/gen-engine-inputs.js, and with --calculus the calculus path with the legacy interpreter. Exegra/Impl/ is what the application runs: Impl/Json.lean (the in-place writer, write'_eq), Impl/Semantics.lean (the definition with that writer) and Impl/Refinement.lean (runAnyValue_eq and runAny_eq, axioms pinned with #guard_msgs). Format 1 (Semantics, Properties, Lift, Explore, Prove, Temporal, Impl/Expr) was removed (WG-5681).",
        "trusts": [
          "trusted-lean-kernel",
          "trusted-lean-compiler-runtime",
          "trusted-emscripten-toolchain",
          "trusted-wasm-engine"
        ],
        "language": "lean"
      },
      "unknowns": [
        "Format 2 is defined in formal/Exegra/Format2.lean for the constructs built so far (instances, routes, resources and resource faults, windows, workloads, links, timers, request lifecycle and measured outputs); formal/Exegra/Resource.lean proves the held-units and queue-bound invariants of a settled resource, the strict FIFO queue of a collapse included. Not defined yet: crash-instance with incarnations, meters and cost (planned: WG-4741).",
        "The determinism property over seeds is not stated as a theorem for format 2 yet (planned: WG-4741).",
        "Exegra.Json.parse is a partial definition, shared by the definition and the implementation, so it is outside every theorem (planned: WG-4797). Inputs outside the behaviour calculus run the legacy format-2 interpreter, which parses each expression at every evaluation (planned: WG-4798)."
      ],
      "tags": [
