    {
      "id": "lts-foundation",
      "name": "Transition-system foundation (Lean 4)",
      "parent": "semantic-core",
      "category": "logical",
      "runsIn": "both",
      "purpose": "The mathematical object every guarantee is a proposition about (docs/foundations.md, section 3 and milestones M1 and M2): labelled transition systems as relations, reachability, invariants with no bound on runs, inductive invariants, vacuity, bounded reachability, closed-set certificates, the bridge from the generic search to transition-system invariants, a minimal well-scoped guarded-command calculus over Int whose semantics is a transition system, and the typed behaviour calculus (machines, events, guards, effects) whose semantics is a transition system with snapshot reads and an explicit error state.",
      "responsibilities": [
        "Prove unbounded safety from an inductive invariant (Exegra.Core.LTS.safe_of_inductive) and from a closed-set certificate checked by kernel evaluation (checkClosed_sound)",
        "State vacuity explicitly: with no initial state every predicate is an invariant (invariant_of_no_init), so an invariant result is only meaningful together with NonVacuous",
        "Distinguish bounded from unbounded results: an invariant is a bounded invariant at every depth (invariant_iff_all_bounds) and no single depth suffices (Exegra.Core.Examples.bounded_not_unbounded)",
        "Carry results of the generic search to transition systems: a holds result is an unbounded invariant of every system the search covers (invariant_of_search) and a counterexample refutes the invariant of every system it is sound for (not_invariant_of_search)",
        "Give the guarded-command calculus a semantics in which out-of-range variables cannot be written, updates read the state before the step (Cmd.exec_get) and steps are labelled by command",
        "Give Exegra's behaviours (machines with control states and typed variables, events with payloads, guards, effects that set the machine's own variables, emit events and set or cancel the machine's own timers, and the scenario's event and transition faults) a typed calculus over Int, Bool and String whose semantics is a transition system (Exegra.Core.Behav.Sys.lts): references scoped by Fin, so a missing variable or machine cannot be written; integer arithmetic as format 2 computes it, each arithmetic node checked against the declared integer type format 2's staticTy gives it, % truncated and typed / truncating; faults all-or-nothing per fault through a mask chosen in the initial state and kept (mask_const), with drop, lose, delay, retry, duplicate, enable, disable and inject faults, occurrences counted per event and windows; times at or beyond 2^53 as the error timeBound and no delivery after stopAt; division by zero, inexact untyped division, out-of-range operands and results and reads past the end of a state as errors, never default values or other values; a failing delivery leads to an explicit error state with no successor (err_deadlocked), and every system has an initial state (lts_nonVacuous)",
        "Prove that under snapshot reads a delivery does not depend on the order in which its winners fire: the machines after phase 2 (Exegra.Core.Behav.phase2_perm), the emitted blocks up to permutation (phase2_perm_emitted), the winners of every step on distinct machines (winners_nodup), and the whole successor configuration, pending list included, equal in every order (deliver_perm), emitted entries being inserted by time in machine-index order as format 2's insertE inserts them; and show on the [a, b] / [b, a] example that snapshot reads give one verdict (f4_ab_safe, f4_ba_safe) where the calculus's model of today's next-configuration reads gives two (f4_next_reads_differ)",
        "Prove that the shape of a configuration (one state per machine, a control state of the machine, one value of the declared sort per variable) is invariant from a shaped initial configuration (Exegra.Core.Behav.shaped_invariant) and that no step from a reachable configuration then fails with shape (shape_unreachable), and that the static sort of an expression is sound for evaluation (Expr.eval_fits)",
        "Define a decidable well-formedness of systems (Exegra.Core.Behav.Sys.WF) and prove that no step of a well-formed system fails with sort from a reachable state (sort_unreachable), that times stay in order and below 2^53 (time_invariant) and that the queue stays sorted by time (sorted_invariant)",
        "Give the calculus an executable scheduler (Exegra.Core.Behav.Exec.step, Exec.run) computed with the executable delivery, which deliver_iff_step proves equal to the declarative relation Step, and prove it is one scheduler of the relation: every committed step is a step of Sys.lts labelled by the delivered entry and its draws (Exec.step_sound), a failed step leads to the error state (Exec.step_failed), it stops only where the relation has no step on a sorted queue (Exec.idle_deadlocked, Exec.stopped_deadlocked) and every state of a run is reachable (run_reach); and give it a successor function equal to the relation's steps (successors_complete, successors_sound), whose search system covers and is sound for the relation (Exec.covers, Exec.sound), so Gen.search results are theorems about the calculus (Exec.holds_invariant, Exec.cex_refutes)",
        "Cover counted instances, targets and replies (machines are instances; aggregates count, sum, min and max over instances read from the start-of-step snapshot; entries carry their sender and a reply addresses it), and prove that the verdict of a safety property does not depend on the order in which machines are listed (Exegra.Core.Behav.invariant_rename)",
        "Elaborate format-2 engine input into the calculus (Exegra.V2.Elab.elab), refusing inputs outside the fragment, ill-typed or with non-integer numbers, with every accepted input well-formed (elab_wf) and every state of its calculus run reachable (Exegra.V2.runB_reach); explore the calculus with unbounded holds results and real counterexamples (explore_holds_invariant, explore_cex_real) and monitor finite runs for eventually, never, precedes and bounded response (monitor_sound)"
      ],
      "unknowns": [
        "The guarded-command calculus (Calculus.lean) is single-sorted (Int) with no events, timers, faults or time.",
        "The behaviour calculus (Behav.lean, BehavExec.lean, BehavRename.lean, BehavCheck.lean) does not cover resources, links, workloads, keyed instances or routes, sample effects (a variable set from a draw; only delays are drawn), option types, measured outputs or resource faults. Elab.elab refuses inputs that use them as outOfFragment; they keep running on the legacy format-2 interpreter (Exegra.V2.traceOf), which computes with Float, their trace says \"semantics\": \"legacy\", and no theorem is about them (milestone M2b).",
        "The calculus explorer's temporal check reads the format-2 encoding of each step (ProveB.lean, Occ) to match occurrences; that encoding is shared with traceOfB but no theorem relates a matched occurrence to the calculus label of the step.",
        "Some format-2 values are errors in the calculus: untyped arithmetic with an operand or result outside 2^53 - 1 and untyped / with a remainder, == and != across sorts (refused as ill-typed), mixed string and number +, and a payload field read at a sort it does not fit; > and >= are encoded as swapped < and <= (which error is reported can change), and the error reported can depend on the order of the winners. The legacy interpreter and the Rust oracle report the same errors for elaborated inputs (differential.js --calculus).",
        "Faults are chosen all-or-nothing per fault (one mask per run), not per occurrence. There are no traces, observations, fairness or composition yet (milestones M5 and M6), and no k-induction (M4).",
        "The calculus explorer that exegra prove runs uses Gen.search, whose visited set hashes with an opaque hash, so a generated proof file discharges its verdict with native_decide and trusts the compiler. The list-based Gen.searchL returns the same result (search_eq_searchL) and reduces in the kernel (Exec.cex_refutesL, Exec.holds_invariantL), but its membership test is linear in the visited states, so generated proof files do not use it.",
        "Semantic completeness (invariant_iff_exists_inductive) is over arbitrary predicates; inductive invariants expressible in the calculus's expressions need not exist."
      ],
      "implementation": {
        "status": "manual",
        "modules": [
          "formal/Exegra/Core/Behav.lean",
          "formal/Exegra/Core/BehavExamples.lean",
          "formal/Exegra/Core/BehavExec.lean",
          "formal/Exegra/Core/BehavRename.lean",
          "formal/Exegra/Core/Calculus.lean",
          "formal/Exegra/Core/Examples.lean",
          "formal/Exegra/Core/LTS.lean",
          "formal/Exegra/Core/Search.lean",
          "formal/Exegra/Elab.lean"
        ],
        "notes": "formal/Exegra/Core/LTS.lean (the theory), Calculus.lean (the guarded-command calculus), Search.lean (the bridge from Exegra.Gen.search), Examples.lean (positive and adversarial examples: counter_le, ne_neg_one_not_inductive, ne_neg_one_invariant, bounded_not_unbounded, vacuous_false, mod3_in_range), Behav.lean (the behaviour calculus of M2.1 and M2.2: typed values, arithmetic sorts and expressions, transitions, timers, faults, Sys.lts, Sys.WF, phase2_perm, phase2_perm_emitted, winners_nodup, deliver_perm, mask_const, deliver_ok_form, deliver_error_form, shaped_invariant, shape_unreachable, time_invariant, sorted_invariant, Expr.eval_fits, Expr.eval_ne_sort, sort_unreachable), BehavExec.lean (the scheduler of M2.3: Exec.step, Exec.run, Exec.successors, Exec.enum, Exec.gen, Exec.step_sound, Exec.step_failed, Exec.idle_deadlocked, Exec.stopped_deadlocked, Exec.step_live, run_reach, successors_complete, successors_sound, Exec.covers, Exec.sound, Exec.holds_invariant, Exec.cex_refutes), BehavRename.lean (machine renaming: invariant_rename, step_rename, step_unrename), BehavCheck.lean (exploreB, explore_holds_invariant, explore_cex_real, TForm, FinSat, monitor, monitor_sound), BehavExamples.lean (agg_last_safe, agg_first_safe, agg_next_reads_differ, reply_reaches_requester, reply_without_sender_fails, ctr_le, choice_safe, f4_ab_safe, f4_ba_safe, f4_ab_safe_closed, f4_wf, f4_exec_same_machines, f4_next_reads_differ, f4_nonVacuous, f4Init_shaped, f4_ab_no_shape, divZero_counterexample, mod_truncates, typed_div_truncates, untyped_div_inexact, u8_overflow, emitted_inserted_by_time, emission_order_irrelevant, timer_then_cancel, timer_reset, drop_second_delivery, drop_fault_optional); axioms pinned with #guard_msgs. The files sit inside formal/, which formal-semantics also declares. Checked by lake build in .github/workflows/check.yml. formal/Exegra/Elab.lean (Elab.elab, elab_wf, runB_reach) elaborates format-2 input into the calculus.",
        "trusts": [
